{%- comment -%}
  The article index starts with the shared Search country groups above, then
  adds adjectival and review-specific tag aliases. Display labels stay the same
  in both interfaces. No country-to-producer relationship is encoded here.

  Caribbean also absorbs the territories that articles tag by their own name
  but that belong under that umbrella: Guyana, its Demerara marques, and the
  French West Indies (Martinique, Guadeloupe, Marie Galante), plus Haiti and
  Grenada. Before this, rum tagged only with one of those reached no country
  tab at all, and the producers behind it were listed nowhere.

  Where the line was drawn, and why:

  - Mainland Latin America stays out. Nicaragua, Panama, Venezuela and Belize
    keep their own identity, in keeping with Guatemala already standing as its
    own country above. Folding a neighbour in while Guatemala stands alone
    would contradict the list rather than extend it.
  - Reunion, Mauritius and Madeira stay out. They are Indian Ocean and
    Atlantic, not Caribbean, however similar the rhum. Their producers stay
    with their own countries: Isautier and Savanna remain French.
  - Guyana and Demerara go in even though Guyana is mainland South America.
    Its rum is West Indian in every sense the site uses the word, and the two
    tags are used interchangeably across the corpus.

  Adding a tag here decides only which articles reach a country tab. Which
  producers are then listed under it is a separate question, answered by the
  producer origins below - a producer still has to be declared as belonging to
  the country before it appears there. Both halves are needed: an origin is a
  filter over articles the country already matched, never a source of them.
{%- endcomment -%}
