{%- comment -%}
  Country of origin per producer, used only by the Article Index to keep a
  producer from surfacing under a country it has nothing to do with. An
  article that compares producers across borders carries every country tag
  and every producer tag, so country membership and producer membership have
  to be checked against each other rather than independently.

  Each definition is "Label::country", using the country labels above. This is
  where the producer itself is from, and it is one country. It is not a list of
  the countries a producer's drinks come from: an independent bottler is its
  own home and nothing else, so Velier is Italy rather than the six regions it
  bottles, Blackadder is UK rather than UK and Japan, and Moon Import is Italy
  rather than Italy and UK.

  That works because origins are consulted only for an article naming several
  countries, where it is genuinely unclear which producer belongs to which. An
  article naming one country speaks for itself and lists every producer on it.
  So a bottler's next release from a country it has never touched lists itself,
  with no edit here - which is the whole point of not enumerating.

  Two entries name several countries, and both are producers whose articles
  always name several countries, so nothing else can place them: Kuheiji is
  Japan and France for its Burgundy domaine, and Penfolds is Australia, France
  and China for the wines it makes in each. They are the exception, not a
  pattern to follow.

  Bielle is Caribbean rather than France because Marie-Galante, though legally
  French, is filed by the site under the Caribbean group above.

  Nobilis is Denmark, which has no tab of its own. That is correct rather than
  a mistake: it simply means an ambiguous article never claims Nobilis for one
  of its countries, while its ordinary single-country reviews list it as usual.
  A producer omitted here entirely is unrestricted, so a new producer is never
  hidden by default. Search does not read this field.
{%- endcomment -%}
